**Why Type 3 beats Type 2**
The Two case is real. He bought classmates' friendship as a kid, he goes out of his way to anticipate his wife's needs, and his mission is making viewers "feel seen." But at every costly choice he put his worth in visible success and his fear in public failure:
- After the 2007 housing crash he started a car-rental business so he could keep his Lamborghini and, in his words, "the appearance that I was a lot more successful than I actually was."
- At 30 he dreaded a tombstone reading "the biggest failure that has ever existed," and someone advised him to change his name.
- He says he couldn't feel he belonged "until I reached a certain level of success objectively."

This pattern shows up years before YouTube rewarded it. In 2010 he told Mother Jones he'd "always wanted a reality show." In 2014 he said he moved to LA because "you need two things, money and fame." Seven, Eight, Four and Six were also tested and rejected; the reasons are in the file.

**What the reviewers will see**
- **Sensitive-allegations flag is on.** That sends the draft to the full six-reviewer panel. It covers the fraud conviction, the 2023 actors' allegations, and the childhood violence he mentions only once.
- **Fraud-case figures.** The criminal sentence was a $10,000 fine, $44,399.51 restitution and five years' probation. The $37,500 was a separate 2015 civil settlement, which the NYT merged with the fine. Mann himself says "$40,000."
- **Unverified Ponzi episode.** He names the friend who ran the scheme he says wiped him out in 2014. There's no public record of it, so the drafter should attribute it to his account and not name the man.
- **Check quotes against the audio.** The How I Built This quotes come from a machine transcript, so each one needs a re-listen at its timestamp before it goes in the article.

